<!--
    ELF GOT import-call binding (`option peimportcall on|off`).

    Issue: a `-fno-plt` build (or a linker-relaxed `call *slot(%rip)`) calls an
    import through its GLOB_DAT slot in `.got`; there is no PLT stub to name.
    The call lifts to a CALLIND through a global, so it renders as
    `(*dat_3fb0)()` with no name, no prototype and no no-return flow effect: a
    function ending in `abort()`/`exit()` runs on into the next function until
    funcboundflow truncates it.

    Stage / sub-stage: P1 external-refinement (`kuna-analysis`
    `loader/elf_plt.rs` names the `.got` slots of undefined function symbols no
    PLT stub jumps through, and `loader/kuna_peimportcall.rs` paints them
    `externref`) feeding the P2/P4 deindirect seam, whose restart re-flows the
    function with the import's no-return flag.

    P0 assertion: `option peimportcall on|off`.

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture; both passes load the
    real fixture `elf_got_noreturn_x86_64` (source `elf_got_noreturn_x86_64.c`,
    gcc -O2 -fPIC -pie -fno-plt). `check` ends in a GOT call to `abort`, `die`
    in a GOT call to `exit`, and `pick` reads a function pointer from a
    `.data.rel.ro` table filled by the same kind of relocation outside `.got`.

    Pass 1 (`option peimportcall off`) reproduces the BUG: both calls are
    unnamed and carry the funcboundflow truncation warning.
    Pass 2 (`option peimportcall on`) is the FIX: `abort()` and `exit(2)` are
    named and no-return, and the warning raised before the restart is gone.
    The table read in `pick` stays a table read in both passes.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
